# How is a QALY actually calculated?
prompt: "Where do the utility weights (u_t) come from — who decides that a state is worth 0.7? What are the main elicitation methods: time trade-off, standard gamble, EQ-5D? How do we turn a health profile over many years into a single QALY number?"
comments: {"repoId":"R_kgDOUIbP1A","category":"Announcements","categoryId":"DIC_kwDOUIbP1M4DEv5x"}
next: {"title":"The theoretical foundations: where does the QALY model come from?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/understanding-the-qaly/the-theoretical-foundations-where-does.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

chapter: "Eliciting the Weights"

## Whose Judgment, Whose Weight?
level: advanced
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 15
    'y':
      - 0
      - 1
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 250
      w: 920
      h: 430

    axes ax x_label "Years lived" y_label "Utility (u_t)" hidden true
    curve line_full dashed #8f887c expr "1" x_from 0 x_to 13 hidden true
    label label_full "Full health (u=1)" right attach_to line_full font_size 22 hidden true
    curve line_dead dashed #8f887c expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 13 hidden true
    label label_dead "Dead (u=0)" right attach_to line_dead font_size 22 hidden true
    path seg1 thick #b5482e points [[0,0.75],[5,0.75]] data true hidden true
    path drop1 dashed #3d3833 points [[5,0.75],[5,0.5]] data true hidden true
    path seg2 thick #d0865f points [[5,0.5],[10,0.5]] data true hidden true
    path drop2 dashed #3d3833 points [[10,0.5],[10,0]] data true hidden true
    group trajectory members ["seg1","drop1","seg2","drop2"] hidden true
    label val_075 "u = 0.75" above #b5482e attach_to seg1 hidden true
    label val_050 "u = 0.50" below #d0865f attach_to seg2 hidden true
    text qmark "?" #8a5fa8 at.data [5,0.62] font_size 48 hidden true
    person node_patient "" width 90 height 90 hidden true
    label label_patient "Patient" below attach_to node_patient hidden true
    group src_patient members ["node_patient","label_patient"] in sources hidden true
    shape node_public1 shape circle radius 22 hidden true
    shape node_public2 right node_public1 gap -14 shape circle radius 22 hidden true
    shape node_public3 left node_public1 gap -14 shape circle radius 22 hidden true
    group public_cluster members ["node_public1","node_public2","node_public3"] hidden true
    label label_public "General public" above attach_to public_cluster hidden true
    group src_public members ["public_cluster","label_public"] in sources hidden true
    shape node_clinician shape circle radius 34 hidden true
    text clin_cross "+" #2f6b8f at.ref node_clinician font_size 32 hidden true
    group clinician_icon members ["node_clinician","clin_cross"] hidden true
    label label_clinician "Clinician" below attach_to clinician_icon hidden true
    group src_clinician members ["clinician_icon","label_clinician"] in sources hidden true
    row sources gap 90 fit.h 210 fit.w 900 fit.x 60 fit.y 30 hidden true
    label label_dominant "Dominant source" above #8a5fa8 attach_to public_cluster gap 55 hidden true
    mark mark_075 around val_075 kind box hidden true
    shape rater_25 below-left public_cluster gap 15 #8a5fa8 shape circle radius 14 hidden true
    shape rater_adapted below-right public_cluster gap 15 #8a5fa8 shape circle radius 14 hidden true
    arrow elicitor_arrow thick #8a5fa8 from.anchor right from.ref rater_25 to.anchor left to.ref rater_adapted hidden true
    label elicitor_label "Elicitor effect" below #8a5fa8 attach_to elicitor_arrow font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Whose Judgment, Whose Weight?"

Every QALY calculation rests on a number nobody watches you assign — the utility weight u_t — and today we open that black box.
    draw ax line_full label_full line_dead label_dead parallel true

    pause 0.3

Here is the patient's trajectory without treatment: ten years of remaining life, quality declining as the hip deteriorates.
    draw trajectory

    pause 0.4

For the first five years the patient rates his own state at 0.75 — mobile, some pain, still working.
    draw val_075

    highlight seg1 val_075 effect glow

By year six the pain has worsened and mobility is lost; the same patient now anchors his state at 0.50.
    draw val_050

    highlight seg2 val_050 effect glow

    hide label_full label_dead

Where did 0.75 and 0.50 come from?
    draw qmark

Not from a clinical chart — from someone's judgment about how bad the state is compared to full health and death.
    highlight line_full line_dead effect glow

    arrow qmark_arrow dashed #8a5fa8 from.anchor bottom from.ref qmark to.anchor top to.ref public_cluster

    pause 0.3

Three populations can supply that judgment: the patient living the state, a sample of the general public imagining it, or the treating clinician assessing it from outside.
    draw sources

Most standard QALY estimates — including the ones regulators use — draw on general population values, not patient experience, because policy has to compare across diseases using one common yardstick.
    draw label_dominant

    highlight src_public effect circle

    erase qmark_arrow

That choice is not neutral: a healthy twenty-five-year-old imagining paraplegia and a patient adapting to it after two years rarely give the same number.
    draw rater_25 rater_adapted elicitor_arrow elicitor_label parallel true

    point at.ref rater_25 gesture tap

    point at.ref rater_adapted gesture tap

    pause 0.3

    camera on ["seg1","val_075"] zoom fit

This single figure, 0.75, is therefore a compressed social judgment — and the next parts show exactly how it gets produced.
    draw mark_075

    camera reset true

## Time Trade-Off: Trading Years for Health
level: advanced
vars: {"xf":7}

    shape bar1 #b5482e shape rect x 500 y 550 width 680 height 100 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    label label_bar1 "Hip state (u=?)" above attach_to bar1 hidden true
    shape bar2 #2f6b8f at.anchor left at.offset [0,-170] at.ref bar1 anchor left shape rect width 392 height 100 bind.width 68*xf style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    label label_bar2 "Full health, 8.5 years" below attach_to bar2 gap 30 hidden true
    arrow arr_shrink #8f887c from.x 650 from.y 455 to.x 550 to.y 455 hidden true
    arrow arr_grow #8f887c from.x 650 from.y 475 to.x 750 to.y 475 hidden true
    text eq_sign "=" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 465 font_size 70 hidden true
    math eq tex "u = \\frac{x}{t}" size 34 steps ["u = \\frac{8.5}{10} = 0.85"] colors.t #b5482e colors.x #2f6b8f x 500 y 215 hidden true
    mark result_box around eq kind box hidden true
    text label_bar2_moving "Full health, ? years" x 300 y 298 font_size 22 hidden true
    text label_bar1_solved "Hip state (u=0.85)" x 500 y 628 font_size 22 hidden true
    label annot_exchange "Years ↔ health" right #8f887c attach_to eq gap 40 hidden true
    label label_negative "x < 0" above #b5482e attach_to bar_negative hidden true
    shape bar_negative dashed #b5482e at.anchor left at.ref bar2 anchor right shape rect width 150 height 100 hidden true

    card title "Time Trade-Off: Trading Years for Health"

Now that we have seen why someone must assign u_t, the time trade-off method asks that someone directly to price health against time.
    draw bar1

Present the respondent with ten years lived in the state we are valuing — moderate residual pain, restricted mobility.
    draw label_bar1

    point at.ref bar1 gesture circle

Against it, offer fewer years in full health, and ask which the respondent prefers.
    draw bar2 label_bar2_moving arr_shrink arr_grow

The interviewer moves the full-health bar back and forth — more years, fewer years — until the respondent is indifferent between the two.
    blocking false

    animate xf 5 duration 1.1

    animate xf 9 duration 1.3

Indifference here lands at 8.5 years of full health traded for 10 years in the pain state.
    animate xf 8.5 duration 1.6

    hide label_bar2_moving

    show label_bar2

    draw eq_sign

    pause 0.3

The utility weight falls straight out of that trade: u equals the years of full health accepted divided by the years in the impaired state.
    draw eq

8.5 divided by 10 gives u=0.85 — exactly the weight we will use for the hip-replacement outcome in the final part.
    step target eq in_place true

    hide label_bar1

    show label_bar1_solved

    draw result_box

    camera center.ref eq zoom 2.2

    pause 0.6

    camera reset true

    morph eq tex "u = \\frac{x}{t}" duration 1

The logic generalizes to any duration t: respondents sacrifice some years to escape the state, and the fraction they keep is its utility.
    highlight eq part t

    draw annot_exchange

This method assumes people can meaningfully trade years of life against years of health, a premise the theory lecture will interrogate.
    focus bar1 bar2

    draw bar_negative label_negative parallel true

Time trade-off is intuitive and widely used, but it breaks down for states people rate as worse than death, where x would have to go negative.
    highlight bar2 effect pulse

## Standard Gamble and EQ-5D: Two More Roads to u_t
level: advanced
vars: {"p":0.5}
use: decision_tree
with:
    root:
      id: choice
      type: decision
      label: Elicit u_t
      children: [{label: Certain, node: {id: certain, type: terminal, label: Side-effect state}}, {label: Gamble, node: {id: gamble, type: chance, label: Gamble, children: [{label: Full health, probability: 0.5, node: {id: full, type: terminal, label: Full health}}, {label: Death, probability: 0.5, node: {id: death, type: terminal, label: Death}}]}}]
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 290
      w: 460
      h: 370

    path slider_track #8f887c points [[60,240],[440,240]] hidden true
    text slider_lo "p = 0" #8f887c x 60 y 215 font_size 20 hidden true
    text slider_hi "p = 1" #8f887c x 440 y 215 font_size 20 hidden true
    shape slider_marker #2f6b8f shape circle x 250 y 240 radius 12 bind.x "60 + p*380" style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    label slider_value "p = {p:2}" above attach_to slider_marker hidden true
    mark indiff_mark around slider_marker kind circle hidden true
    label indiff_text "p* = 0.60 → u_t" below #b5482e attach_to slider_marker hidden true
    pieces eq5d x 560 y 430 width 380 height 340 n 3 rows 5 of grid hidden true
    label row_label_mobility "Mobility" left attach_to eq5d_1 hidden true
    label row_label_selfcare "Self-care" left attach_to eq5d_4 hidden true
    label row_label_activities "Activities" left attach_to eq5d_7 hidden true
    label row_label_pain "Pain" left attach_to eq5d_10 hidden true
    label row_label_anxiety "Anxiety" left attach_to eq5d_13 hidden true
    group row_labels members ["row_label_mobility","row_label_selfcare","row_label_activities","row_label_pain","row_label_anxiety"] hidden true
    label col_header_1 "None" above attach_to eq5d_1 hidden true
    label col_header_2 "Moderate" above attach_to eq5d_2 hidden true
    label col_header_3 "Severe" above attach_to eq5d_3 hidden true
    group col_headers members ["col_header_1","col_header_2","col_header_3"] hidden true
    label profile_code "Profile: 1-1-2-2-1" left #b5482e attach_to profile_to_table hidden true
    shape lookup_box below eq5d gap 110 #3d3833 shape rect width 200 height 100 hidden true
    label lookup_title "Value set" left attach_to lookup_box hidden true
    label lookup_value "u = 0.60" right #b5482e attach_to lookup_box hidden true
    arrow profile_to_table #2f6b8f from.anchor bottom from.ref eq5d to.anchor top to.ref lookup_box curved false hidden true
    group right_panel members ["eq5d","col_headers","row_labels","lookup_box","lookup_title","profile_to_table","lookup_value","profile_code"] fit.h 480 fit.w 460 fit.x 520 fit.y 170 hidden true

    card title "Standard Gamble & EQ-5D"

Having built one weight from trading time, we turn to two more elicitation routes, starting with the standard gamble.
    draw node_choice label_choice

One arm is certain: live the rest of life in the drug's side-effect state.
    draw edge_choice_certain branchlabel_choice_certain node_certain label_certain

The other arm is a gamble — probability p of full health, probability 1 minus p of immediate death.
    draw edge_choice_gamble branchlabel_choice_gamble node_gamble label_gamble edge_gamble_full branchlabel_gamble_full node_full label_full edge_gamble_death branchlabel_gamble_death node_death label_death

    pause 0.3

    draw slider_track slider_lo slider_hi slider_marker slider_value

The interviewer raises and lowers p until the respondent is indifferent between the sure state and the gamble.
    animate p 0.85 root.children.1.node.children.0.probability 0.85 root.children.1.node.children.1.probability 0.15 duration 1.2

    animate p 0.3 root.children.1.node.children.0.probability 0.3 root.children.1.node.children.1.probability 0.7 duration 1.1

    animate p 0.6 root.children.1.node.children.0.probability 0.6 root.children.1.node.children.1.probability 0.4 duration 1.1

    camera center.ref slider_marker zoom 2.3

Indifference here occurs at p=0.60 — and that probability itself becomes the utility weight.
    draw indiff_mark indiff_text

    pause 0.3

Standard gamble is theoretically the purest measure, because it embeds risk directly, the same currency expected-utility theory is built on — the subject of the next lecture.
    highlight node_gamble node_full node_death edge_gamble_full edge_gamble_death effect glow

    camera reset true

EQ-5D sidesteps direct elicitation for this patient altogether, describing health through five dimensions: mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort, anxiety or depression.
    draw eq5d col_headers row_labels

    pause 0.3

The side-effect state scores moderate problems on activities and pain, no problems on self-care — a five-digit profile, not a number yet.
    highlight eq5d_1 eq5d_4 eq5d_8 eq5d_11 eq5d_13 effect glow color #b5482e

    draw profile_code

That profile is looked up in a value set, itself built from thousands of prior time-trade-off or discrete-choice interviews with the general population.
    draw profile_to_table lookup_box lookup_title

The lookup returns u=0.60 — matching the standard gamble result, though the two methods do not always agree so neatly.
    draw lookup_value

    pause 0.3

Three different procedures, three numbers converging near the same weight — the rest of the calculation now just needs those weights and the time patients spend in each state.
    arrow bracket curved #8a5fa8 from.ref slider_marker to.anchor left to.ref lookup_box
    label bracket_label "u_t ≈ 0.60" above #8a5fa8 attach_to bracket

chapter: "Building the Number"

## From Profile to a Single Number
level: advanced
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 13
    'y':
      - 0
      - 1.1
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 150
      w: 820
      h: 420

    math eq details "Willingness-to-pay thresholds built on this sum vary by country: NICE in the UK used £20,000–£30,000 per QALY gained, raised to £25,000–£35,000 from April 2026." tex "QALY = \\sum_t u_t \\times \\Delta t" size 34 colors {"u_t":"#2f6b8f","\\Delta t":"#b5482e"} x 500 y 660 hidden true
    axes ax x_label Years y_label "Utility (uₜ)" hidden true
    curve zero expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 13 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    curve baseline_1 #8f887c expr "0.75" x_from 0 x_to 5 hidden true
    curve baseline_2 #8f887c expr "0.50" x_from 5 x_to 10 hidden true
    region region_baseline_1 x_from 0 x_to 5 between ["baseline_1","zero"] style.fill #8f887c style.fill_style wash hidden true
    region region_baseline_2 x_from 5 x_to 10 between ["baseline_2","zero"] style.fill #8f887c style.fill_style wash hidden true
    curve optionA #2f6b8f expr "0.85" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    region region_A x_from 0 x_to 10 between ["optionA","zero"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash hidden true
    curve optionB #b5482e expr "0.60" x_from 0 x_to 13 hidden true
    region region_B x_from 0 x_to 13 between ["optionB","zero"] style.fill #b5482e style.fill_style wash hidden true
    label label_baseline_area "Baseline: 6.25" above-right #8f887c attach_to region_baseline_2 font_size 28 hidden true
    label label_A_area "Hip: 8.5" above #2f6b8f attach_to region_A font_size 28 hidden true
    label label_B_area "Drug: 7.8" below #b5482e attach_to region_B font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch calc_baseline top-right work ["5 × 0.75 = 3.75","5 × 0.50 = 2.50","Total = 6.25 QALYs"] font_size 22 hidden true
    scratch calc_A top-right work ["10 × 0.85 = 8.5 QALYs"] font_size 22 hidden true
    scratch calc_B top-right work ["13 × 0.60 = 7.8 QALYs"] font_size 22 hidden true
    box n_judgment "Judgment" width 150 height 70 in pipeline_row hidden true
    box n_elicit "Elicitation" width 150 height 70 in pipeline_row hidden true
    box n_ut "uₜ" width 150 height 70 in pipeline_row hidden true
    box n_sum "Σ uₜ·Δt" width 150 height 70 in pipeline_row hidden true
    box n_qaly "QALY" width 150 height 70 in pipeline_row hidden true
    row pipeline_row gap 30 fit.h 115 fit.w 880 fit.x 60 fit.y 15 hidden true
    arrow p_arrow1 n_judgment -> n_elicit hidden true
    arrow p_arrow2 n_elicit -> n_ut hidden true
    arrow p_arrow3 n_ut -> n_sum hidden true
    arrow p_arrow4 n_sum -> n_qaly hidden true

    card title "From Profile to a Single Number"

    draw ax

Now that we have three ways to produce u_t — trade-off, gamble, and descriptive lookup — the final step is turning a whole health profile into one number.
    draw eq

The QALY is simply the sum, over every period of the patient's life, of the utility weight times the time spent at that weight — the area under the health-state curve.
    highlight eq

Untreated, the patient lives five years at u=0.75, then five more at u=0.50 as the hip deteriorates.
    draw baseline_1 baseline_2 region_baseline_1 region_baseline_2 parallel true

    pause 0.3

That profile sums to 3.75 plus 2.5 — 6.25 QALYs, the reference point against which both treatments are judged.
    draw calc_baseline_box calc_baseline_line_1 calc_baseline_line_2 calc_baseline_line_3 label_baseline_area

    point at.ref label_baseline_area gesture circle

    pause 0.4

    erase calc_baseline_box calc_baseline_line_1 calc_baseline_line_2 calc_baseline_line_3

    fade region_baseline_1 region_baseline_2 to 0.15

The hip replacement does not extend life, but it holds quality of life at 0.85 for the full ten years.
    draw optionA region_A parallel true

Ten years times 0.85 gives 8.5 QALYs — over two full QALYs gained purely from quality, with zero extra survival.
    draw calc_A_box calc_A_line_1 label_A_area

    point at.ref label_A_area gesture circle

    pause 0.3

    erase calc_A_box calc_A_line_1

The drug extends survival to thirteen years, but the side effects hold quality down at 0.60 throughout.
    draw optionB region_B parallel true

Thirteen times 0.60 gives 7.8 QALYs.
    draw calc_B_box calc_B_line_1 label_B_area

    point at.ref label_B_area gesture circle

    pause 0.4

    erase calc_B_box calc_B_line_1

    camera on ["region_A","region_B","label_A_area","label_B_area"] zoom fit

Despite three additional years of life, the drug delivers fewer QALYs than the hip replacement — longevity and quality are literally interchangeable in this arithmetic, and here quality wins.
    highlight label_A_area label_B_area

    pause 0.5

    camera reset true

This is the entire mechanics of a QALY: elicit a weight for every distinguishable state a person can be in, multiply each by its duration, and add.
    highlight eq label_baseline_area label_A_area label_B_area

Everything upstream — patient, public, or clinician judgment, captured by time trade-off, standard gamble, or EQ-5D — funnels into that single sum, which is why the same number can look objective while carrying every assumption we just unpacked.
    draw pipeline_row p_arrow1 p_arrow2 p_arrow3 p_arrow4

What you can see now is the whole pipeline: a judgment about a state's worth becomes a weight, the weight becomes an area under a curve, and the area becomes the one figure that decides which of these two treatments looks better on paper.
    highlight eq pipeline_row

## Where next
end_page: true

